From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 22:31:42 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] Exact guest instruction boundaries

A thread that a signal stops, or whose fault the guest resolves, starts again at a guest instruction from the
static registers and the context alone. Not every entry of the RIP table was such a point:
 - the register allocator merges operations of neighbouring instructions (push/push, pop/pop, pop/ret, moves);
 - the host carry is the guest's or its opposite, whichever the last instruction left;
 - the high halves of the AVX registers, the MMX registers, the direction flag and the x87 stack are written to
   the context at the end of a block.
Each entry now says whether it is Exact and what form the carry has; an entry over which the allocator moves an
effect is not Exact, and a merge is only made where nothing that can fault would be reported there. What is
pending is written before every instruction that can fault, the x87 stack before every instruction.
